WEBVTT - The $4.2M Payroll Divert: BEC Wire Redirection (English)

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Tuesday morning, eight-fourteen AM. A finance manager is preparing the final payment for an important business acquisition.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Suddenly, an email arrives that appears to come straight from her CEO, Robert Sterling, asking to wire $4.2 million to an emergency account.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.000
The email looks completely genuine. The regular spam filter raises no alarms. And the deadline is less than ninety minutes away.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Trusting her instincts, the manager paused and dropped the email into TraceXMail. Within a fraction of a second, the disguise fell apart.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
While the name on top said 'Robert Sterling', the hidden postal stamps told the real story.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:35.000
The email was sent from an anonymous computer server located in Sofia, Bulgaria, slipping a fake letter into the inbox.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:50.000
Next, TraceXMail inspected the digital seal. The scammer had created their own domain name replacing the letter 'O' with a number 'zero'.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:05.000
To a human eye reading quickly, they look the same. But the digital seal on that letter belonged to the fake domain.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:20.000
TraceXMail immediately flagged a 98% critical fraud alert, warning the manager to stop before a single dollar was transferred.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
With the payment stopped, the manager clicked one button to download TraceXMail’s complete incident report.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The report contained the overseas server address and the lookalike trick with a certified digital seal for law enforcement.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:14.000
Four point two million dollars stayed safe in the company's account. No guesswork, no panic—just clear, understandable truth.
